WEBVTT
TITLE Arrays Stacks Queues — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
An array is one block of memory cut into equal slots. The index is not stored anywhere — it is arithmetic on the base address.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Address of a[i] = base + i times w, where w is the size of one element in bytes. That is the whole of indexing.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Reading any element costs the same. Inserting in the middle costs n shifts, because the block must stay contiguous.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Two consequences GATE uses: nothing checks the index, and the length is recoverable only where the array type is still visible.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
A two-dimensional array is still one run of memory. Row-major stores whole rows one after another; column-major stores whole columns.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Row-major: base + ((i - L1) times cols + (j - L2)) times w. Column-major swaps the two roles: rows where columns stood.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.000
The mnemonic that never fails: in row-major the second index varies fastest, so consecutive addresses walk along a row.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
GATE 1998: A[1..10][1..15], one location per integer, row-major, first element at 100. The address is 15i + j + 84.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
C does not check array bounds. a[10] on int a[10] compiles; the standard makes reading or writing it undefined behaviour.

10
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.000
a[-1] is the same kind of mistake from the other side: defined pointer arithmetic only inside the object, and here it leaves the object.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.000
An array name becomes a pointer to its first element in almost every expression. sizeof is one of the few exceptions.

12
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
So a function that fills an array must be given its length, and an index that came from user input must be checked by you.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
A stack inserts and removes at one end only. Last in, first out: the newest element is the only one you can reach.

14
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.000
On an array: int a[N]; int top = -1. push writes a[++top]; pop reads a[top--]. Both are O(1).

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Every operation is O(1), and the price is a fixed capacity. Overflow belongs to this implementation, not to the stack idea.

16
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Two boundary states to keep apart: empty is top == -1, full is top == N-1. Mixing them is an off-by-one that costs a mark.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Postfix writes each operator after its two operands, so no parentheses and no precedence rules are needed.

18
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Scan left to right. A number is pushed. An operator pops two, computes, and pushes the result. The first pop is the right operand.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:23.000
GATE 2015 Set 3: 10 5 + 60 6 / * 8 - gives 15, then 10, then 150, then 142. The answer is 142.

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
GATE 2007 asked a different question about the same trace: the top two elements after the first * are 6 and 1, not the final value 2.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Push 1, 2, 3 in that order, and pop whenever you like. The output orders are limited: 1, 2, 3 and 3, 2, 1 are both possible.

22
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Greedy test: push until the next wanted element is on top, then pop it. If it is buried under a larger element, the order is impossible.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:53.000
GATE 1994 offered four orders for the input 1..5. Only 3, 4, 5, 2, 1 works. In 3, 4, 5, 1, 2 the 1 sits under the 2 forever.

24
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
How many are reachable? The Catalan number: 5 for three elements, 14 for four, 42 for five. Equivalently, the order must avoid the pattern 312.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
A queue inserts at the rear and removes at the front. First in, first out: the oldest element is the one you get.

26
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:15.000
In a plain array both indices only move forward, so the slots at the front are never reused after a dequeue.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:23.000
With N = 5: five insertions, three deletions, then one more insertion is refused while three slots sit free. The queue lies about being full.

28
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Two repairs: shift every element down after each dequeue, which is O(n), or wrap the indices, which is O(1). Every real implementation wraps.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Circular queue: rear becomes (rear + 1) mod N, and front advances the same way. The array behaves as a ring.

30
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.000
But now front == rear happens twice: when the queue is empty, and when it has wrapped all the way round. The indices alone cannot tell you which.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Repair one, sacrifice a slot: empty is front == rear, full is (rear + 1) mod N == front, capacity N - 1, count (rear - front + N) mod N.

32
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.000
GATE 2012 asked exactly this: capacity n - 1 in an array of n, so full is (REAR+1) mod n == FRONT and empty is REAR == FRONT. Answer A.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
A queue from two stacks: push onto one stack. To dequeue, if the other stack is empty, pour everything across, then pop from it.

34
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
One dequeue can move n elements, but each element crosses at most once, so m operations cost O(m). That is amortised O(1).

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
The other direction: a stack from two queues. Push onto the first queue. To pop, move all but the last element to the second queue, take the last, swap.

36
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
And two stacks can share one array: grow one from the left and one from the right. Overflow is exactly top1 + 1 == top2.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Address question: fix the lower bound, the storage order and the element size before substituting anything.

38
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Stack or queue question: draw the array with its index variables and run the operations. Never reason from the name of the structure.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Permutation question: run the greedy push-until-possible test on the option you are checking, and count with Catalan if the question asks how many.

40
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
These are the substrate of everything after: linked lists replace the fixed block, heaps are priority queues, BFS is a queue and DFS is a stack.

